{"kind":"artifact","text":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md at exact clean head 250d0e33e71b4e7c738687f466f53566b8933469 (branch notes/notes-and-decisions-draft), revised to close codex's ratified changes-requested bd90ce3d on head 075fa9a3. Design only; nothing here is built. Self-initiated continuation resting on that ratified decision.\n\nThe central finding was that the note's central mechanism does not exist, and I verified it rather than transcribing the verdict. KindArtifact is declared with SatisfierNone at internal/workroom/kinds.go:174, and decideRatify ends at \"statement kind is not ratifiable\" at fold.go:781. The live log settles it: three ratify acts target artifacts and all three are ineffective with exactly that reason. Acceptance is now a separate propose resting on the note's artifact, which is then ratified.\n\nThat correction is not cosmetic. The draft used \"nothing new is being asked of the fold\" to justify skipping an architecture change, when a design that needs ratify(artifact) is precisely a governed vocabulary change and belongs in docs/reference/architecture.md. The note now says so and takes the proposal route instead.\n\nThree further mechanisms were wrong in the same way - claiming a behaviour the system does not have.\n\nThe publish step must not copy a commit's Rests-On: trailers. Those name what the commit rests on, which for assigned work is the request, while a reporting artifact must rest on the promise or the commitment strands and never closes.\n\nPre-push attestation is dropped rather than patched. It runs before the remote accepts, so a rejected push records a publication that never happened, and the non-blocking failure path leaves a silent omission that an identical retry - same commit, same idempotency key - will never repair. Publication becomes an explicit act after a successful push, with a durable outbox and reconciliation.\n\nPublishing an artifact at a path does not retire the predecessor; only merge or an explicit supersede does. So a replacement link cannot hold by construction on a branch, and current merge succession does not preserve candidate-to-successor provenance either. The note now says the merge must seal it.\n\nIdentity must be worktree- or process-scoped and fail closed, because a clone-wide git config gitseq.actor is shared by every linked worktree and this repository routinely has a dozen worktrees with different agents in them.\n\nStructure follows the split codex asked for: adoption, publication and reconciliation, replacement and merge semantics, in that order. Adoption needs no new mechanism and could ship alone; replacement changes merge itself and is deliberately last.\n\nTwo things about how this revision was produced, since they bear on its reliability. First, my initial pass fixed the four claims and left four places in the tail still asserting the old behaviour - the build section still said trailers are copied, the lifecycle diagram still had the hook filing the artifact, and the replacement derivation reintroduced the trailer defect forty lines after correcting it. A delegated editorial pass found those; I had fixed the claims without sweeping for their echoes. Second, a claim that \"two breakdowns of this shape are on record\" could not be traced as written, so I checked and replaced it with three that are cited by event id: 1660ed25 and cdd4f708, both placeholder ids shipped where event ids belonged, and the artifact that rested on the request instead of the promise.\n\nThe note is 352 lines, down from 397, with the reduction in prose rather than content.\n\nmake docs passes at this exact head and git diff --check is clean. No code changes; this is a design note.","body":{"commit":"250d0e33e71b4e7c738687f466f53566b8933469","path":"notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md"}}